public_system.narrative:
New Zealand gives permanent residents universal coverage through a system funded from general taxation and overseen by the Ministry of Health.{{1}} Eligible residents are covered automatically, hospital care is available at little or no cost at the point of use, and the Accident Compensation Corporation funds injury-related care under a no-fault scheme.{{1}} The Pae Ora (Healthy Futures) Act 2022 replaced the district health boards with a single national entity, Health New Zealand (Te Whatu Ora), and created a Māori Health Authority (Te Aka Whai Ora), which was disestablished in 2024 with its functions moved to Health New Zealand and the Ministry.{{1}}

Patients enroll with a general practice, and Health NZ pays practices through 31 primary health organizations a capitation subsidy for each enrolled patient.{{12}} Those subsidies supply about half of a GP's income; copayments, ACC payments and after-hours funding supply the rest.{{1}} Capacity is tight, with more than a third of practices closed to new enrolments in 2024 and about a third of GPs surveyed planning to retire within five years.{{9}}{{10}} Specialist care is reached by referral from primary care, and specialists often work in both the public and private sectors.{{1}}

Governance has been unsettled. A Commissioner, Professor Lester Levy, was appointed in 2024 to stabilize Health New Zealand, and in July 2025 the government re-established a board, with Levy as chair for 12 months.{{2}} Mark Darrow replaced him as chair for a three-year term from 1 May 2026.{{5}} In the 2024/25 Health Survey, 25.5 percent of adults cited the wait for a GP appointment as a barrier, and 14.9 percent did not visit a GP because of cost.{{4}} Waits are improving against the government's health targets, though elective treatment and emergency department stays were still well short of the 95 percent goals in April to June 2026.{{6}}{{7}}

private_system.narrative:
Private care supplements the public system. Trade press reporting of the 2024/25 New Zealand Health Survey puts private insurance at 35 percent of adults and 29 percent of children, and people use it for faster access to elective surgery, specialist consultations and diagnostics.{{1}}{{3}} Private hospitals focus on elective and nonemergency care and are paid through out-of-pocket fees, insurance and ACC reimbursements, and nearly all aged residential care is delivered by the private sector.{{3}}

Southern Cross is the largest name in the market. Southern Cross Medical Care Society holds about 60 percent of the health insurance market by customer numbers, and in the year to 30 June 2026 it paid a record $1.8 billion (US$1.02 billion) in claims and returned to a $146.9 million (US$83.5 million) surplus after two years of deficits.{{2}} Premiums have climbed quickly. Partners Life raised premiums on existing medical policies three times in 2025, by 18, 20 and 23 percent, and Aon projected an 18 percent rise in employer medical plan costs for 2026.{{1}}{{4}} Southern Cross says it expects increases to moderate, and insurers report more members lapsing or raising excesses.{{1}}{{2}}

The government is making more use of private providers. In March 2025 the Health Minister directed Health New Zealand to offer private providers contracts of up to 10 years for elective surgery, and in July 2025 he announced an additional 21,000 elective procedures over the following year through an "Elective Boost" program.{{6}} In 2026 Health New Zealand signed its first long-term contracts with private hospitals, worth about $300 million (US$170 million) over six years, with Southern Cross Healthcare, The Ascot Hospital and Clinic (Allevia) and Mercy Hospital Dunedin, to treat orthopedic backlogs.{{7}}

education.narrative:
Medical education is concentrated at the University of Otago and the University of Auckland, and annual tuition ranges from NZ$20,000 (US$11,400) to NZ$40,000 (US$22,700).{{1}} Auckland's Bachelor of Medicine and Bachelor of Surgery takes six years of full-time study.{{2}} Graduates then complete two years of prevocational training as interns (postgraduate years 1 and 2), with four accredited clinical attachments in each year; doctors who pass the NZREX Clinical examination follow the same training.{{3}}

A third medical school is under construction. In July 2025 the government agreed to establish a medical school at the University of Waikato focused on primary care and rural and provincial practice, committing $82.85 million (US$47.1 million) alongside a $151.86 million (US$86.3 million) contribution from the university.{{4}} It will train 120 additional doctors a year, lifting total medical school places from 639 a year in 2026 to 759 from 2028.{{4}} Construction of its Hamilton building is scheduled for completion in December 2027, ahead of the first cohort in 2028.{{5}}

Overseas-trained doctors have faster routes in. In July 2025 the Medical Council introduced fast-track registration for GPs trained in the United States, Canada and Singapore, processed within two months, and added Chile, Luxembourg and Croatia to its comparable health system pathway.{{6}}

outlook.narrative:
Demand will keep rising as the population ages: Stats NZ data show about 900,000 people aged 65 and over at 30 June 2025, roughly one in six residents.{{1}} Policy aims at access and throughput. Budget 2026 adds a $1.37 billion (US$778 million) annual uplift, funds more planned care, cancer treatment, emergency department events and general practice enrollments, and lowers the eligibility age for bowel screening to 56.{{2}} The April to June 2026 health target results improved on the year before for all five targets measured, though elective treatment (72.6 percent) and emergency department stays (76.3 percent) remain well short of the 95 percent goals.{{8}} On supply, the Waikato medical school will add 120 places a year from 2028, and the Medical Council has shortened registration routes for overseas doctors.{{3}}{{4}}

The constraints are fiscal and structural. Health New Zealand's deficit narrowed from $835 million (US$474 million) in 2024/25 to $369 million (US$210 million) in 2025/26 on the Treasury's consolidated basis, so it is still spending more than it receives.{{10}} The Treasury projects the government's expense-to-GDP ratio falling below 30 percent by 2030/31 under tight future budget allowances, and it counts on financial improvements at Health New Zealand.{{5}} The public system runs on more than 4,000 fragmented information technology systems, and in 2024, 73 percent of New Zealanders said waits for doctors' appointments were too long.{{6}}

Politics adds uncertainty. The 7 November 2026 general election produced no clear winner. Provisional counts give the National-led bloc 67 seats, a narrow majority, the new Opportunity party holds seven, and about 400,000 special votes were still to be counted.{{7}} Final results are due on 27 November, Christopher Luxon leads a caretaker government, and coalition talks will follow.{{7}} The outcome will shape Budget priorities and how far elective surgery continues to move to private providers.

patient_flows.narrative:
New Zealand sends far more patients abroad than it receives. The public route is the High Cost Treatment Pool. A hospital specialist applies, a Health NZ panel reviews the case, and the treatment must be established and unavailable in New Zealand.{{1}} The Ministry said in 2021 that it funds a handful of treatments a year, spending NZD 26.5 million (US$15.1 million) from July 2009 to June 2020.{{2}} One AIA plan, as listed by an insurance broker, pays up to NZD 30,000 (US$17,000) a year for treatment not available at home.{{3}} Health causes remain the most common reason for fundraising on Givealittle, which passed NZD 31.6 million (US$18 million) in donations in the year to June 2025.{{4}}

Proton therapy is not offered in New Zealand,{{5}} and the Ministry funded five New Zealanders for it in the decade to 2019.{{6}} A 2025 report said a child's brain tumor treatment should start within six weeks of surgery, which funding approval can outlast, so some families pay themselves in the United States.{{7}} CAR-T is publicly funded in Australia, the United Kingdom and much of Europe but not in New Zealand.{{8}} Some adults pay for it in Shanghai, where one patient reported about NZD 150,000 (US$85,200).{{9}} Others go to Thailand, Malaysia, India or Türkiye for dental, orthopedic and cosmetic surgery.{{10}}{{11}}

Inbound flows are small. A Pacific Medical Association review of the Ministry of Foreign Affairs and Trade's Medical Treatment Scheme found 53 people treated under its overseas referral component.{{12}}{{13}} US links run through workforce. The Medical Council fast-tracks general practitioners trained in the United States,{{14}} yet only 38.1 percent of doctors from the Americas stay a year after registering.{{15}}
